[{:adr/authoritative_for ["kotoba-lang/rcs の新設と設計根拠" "kotoba-lang/dtn の新設と設計根拠" "「独自だが SMS/RCS とワイヤ互換の telecom 基盤」という設計スコープの境界確定"], :adr/supersedes [], :adr/superseded_by [], :adr/topic "telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite", :adr/related ["orgs/kotoba-lang/phone" "orgs/kotoba-lang/webrtc" "orgs/kotoba-lang/turn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/net" "90-docs/adr/2607084700-kotoba-lang-apn-cloud-itonami-isic-6110.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607011700-kotoba-lang-webrtc.edn"], :adr/status :accepted, :adr/id "adr-2607161743-kotoba-lang-rcs-dtn-independent-telecom-substrate", :adr/doc_type :adr, :adr/last_verified "2026-07-16", :adr/body "---\nid: adr-2607161743-kotoba-lang-rcs-dtn-independent-telecom-substrate\ntitle: \"ADR-2607161743: kotoba-lang/rcs + kotoba-lang/dtn — a carrier-network-non-dependent telecom substrate, wire-compatible with SMS/RCS\"\nstatus: accepted\ndoc_type: adr\ntopic: telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite\nauthoritative: true\nlast_verified: 2026-07-16\nauthoritative_for:\n  - kotoba-lang/rcs の新設と設計根拠\n  - kotoba-lang/dtn の新設と設計根拠\n  - 「独自だが SMS/RCS とワイヤ互換の telecom 基盤」という設計スコープの境界確定\nrelated:\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/phone\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/webrtc\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/turn\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/net\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607084700-kotoba-lang-apn-cloud-itonami-isic-6110.edn\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607011700-kotoba-lang-webrtc.edn\nsupersedes: []\nsuperseded_by: []\n---\n\n# ADR-2607161743: kotoba-lang/rcs + kotoba-lang/dtn — a carrier-network-non-dependent telecom substrate, wire-compatible with SMS/RCS\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-16\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## Problem\n\nThe question put to the substrate was: *「今の kotoba-lang, cloud-itonami に既存の電話通信網、sms, rcs に依存しない独自かつ全く同じ規格の telecom 基盤は設計されている?」*\n\nA repo-wide search returned **no**: neither `cloud-itonami` (the ISIC business-actor\nfleet) nor `kotoba-lang` (the shared domain-kernel org) had a design for an\nindependent telecom substrate. What existed instead all pointed the *other*\ndirection — toward integrating with or governing existing carrier\ninfrastructure, not replacing it:\n\n- `kotoba-lang/phone` models E.164/SIP/CDR/SMS as data records for a\n  community telecom **operator** (`cloud-itonami-isic-6190`), i.e. a business\n  that resells carrier bandwidth.\n- `kotoba-lang/apn` (ADR-2607084700) models optical transport topology for a\n  licensed network **operator** (`cloud-itonami-isic-6110`).\n- `cloud-itonami-isic-6120` governs a spectrum-licensed mobile network\n  operator — still a licensed-carrier business, the opposite of\n  carrier-independence.\n- `kami-engine-sdk-svelte`'s call README states explicitly: *\"Telecom\n  (PSTN/RCS/SIP) — separate track; the `*-compat` actors remain the\n  management plane\"* — i.e. PSTN/RCS integration is delegated to\n  carrier-API-compatible actors (`twilio-compat` etc.), not replaced.\n\nThe user then asked to close this gap with a new design + ADR. Three\nclarifying questions fixed the scope before implementation:\n\n1. **Motivation** (all four selected, in this priority order): disaster/\n   carrier-outage resilience > cost/MVNO-fee avoidance for cloud-itonami\n   actor↔actor and actor↔user notification > IoT/M2M messaging between\n   kotoba-lang actors and kami-engine-embedded devices > censorship/platform\n   independence.\n2. **Transport substrate** (all three selected — a multi-transport design,\n   not a single new physical network): internet-overlay P2P, mesh radio\n   (LoRa/Meshtastic-shaped, unlicensed ISM band), and satellite links. The\n   design must let the same message egress over whichever of these is\n   actually live, not commit to exactly one.\n3. **Interoperability level**: wire-compatible with 3GPP SMS and GSMA RCS\n   Universal Profile — an existing carrier SMS/RCS client must be able to\n   receive/send through a gateway without modification.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Decision 1: two new `.cljc` capability libraries, zero I/O — the house \"records, not wire format\" discipline\n\n`kotoba-lang/rcs` and `kotoba-lang/dtn` are added, following exactly the\npattern `kotoba-lang/phone`/`kotoba-lang/webrtc`/`kotoba-lang/card`/\n`kotoba-lang/swift` already establish: model protocol structures as plain\nEDN + pure constructor/validator functions, zero network I/O, portable\nacross JVM/ClojureScript/SCI/GraalVM. Neither library moves a single byte\nover a wire; a host (browser app, kami-engine device, or a future\nconsuming actor) supplies the actual transport.\n\n- **`kotoba-lang/rcs`** (`kotoba.rcs`) models GSMA RCS Universal Profile\n  (RCC.07) message/session/capability records — chat message, group chat,\n  is-composing indicator, IMDN delivery/read receipt, file-transfer\n  descriptor, capability-discovery result — complementing\n  `kotoba-lang/phone`'s existing SMS record rather than duplicating it.\n  It `require`s `kotoba.phone` and reuses `phone/e164-valid?` for every\n  endpoint validation; it does not reimplement E.164 parsing.\n- **`kotoba-lang/dtn`** (`kotoba.dtn` + `.link` + `.router` + `.gateway`)\n  models RFC 9171 Bundle Protocol Version 7 as the transport-agnostic\n  envelope. A bundle's EID scheme is `dtn:<e164>`, built directly on\n  `kotoba.phone/e164-valid?`. `kotoba.dtn.link` records a neighbor reachable\n  via one of `#{:internet-overlay :mesh-radio :satellite}`.\n  `kotoba.dtn.router/route-decision` is the store-and-forward core: given a\n  bundle and the currently-known links, forward directly if the destination\n  is a reachable neighbor (picking the cheapest live transport by an\n  overridable priority order — cost/latency-first by default, but a\n  resilience-first caller can reorder to prefer mesh/satellite when\n  internet is down), otherwise hold it in store.\n  `kotoba.dtn.gateway` is the literal SMS/RCS interoperability seam:\n  `bundle->sms`/`sms->bundle` round-trip a `kotoba.phone/sms` record\n  through a bundle payload unchanged, so **any host adapter that already\n  serializes a `phone/sms` record to a real carrier SMS PDU works\n  unmodified against DTN-delivered payloads too** — the same wire format,\n  carried over a different transport. `bundle->rcs-shaped`/\n  `rcs-shaped->bundle` do the same for `kotoba.rcs` chat-message-shaped\n  maps.\n\n### Decision 2: `dtn` does not take a hard dependency on `rcs`\n\n`kotoba.dtn.gateway`'s RCS-facing functions check for the `:rcs/message-id`\nkey structurally (duck-typed) rather than `require`-ing `kotoba-lang/rcs`.\nThe two libraries were built in parallel by independent agents specifically\nto avoid a build-order coupling; keeping the dependency structural instead\nof nominal preserves that independence permanently, not just during the\ninitial build. This mirrors `koe`'s \"host injects every concrete\ncapability\" discipline applied at the library-to-library seam instead of\nthe library-to-host seam.\n\n### Decision 3: honestly-scoped subsets — explicit non-claims, not silent gaps\n\nNeither library claims full spec coverage; each documents its boundary in\nits own namespace docstring, following this workspace's sourcing-honesty\ndiscipline (the same discipline `kotoba-lang/apn`'s RWA solver uses:\n\"single-link-deviation heuristic, explicitly not full k-shortest-paths\").\n\n- `kotoba-lang/rcs` excludes actual SIP/MSRP/CPIM transport framing, GSMA\n  IR.92/IR.94 network registration, and TLS/IPsec transport security.\n- `kotoba-lang/dtn` excludes CBOR wire encoding, block CRC, bundle\n  fragmentation, and BPSec (RFC 9172) security blocks. Its router is a\n  **direct-neighbor-or-store heuristic**, explicitly not full Contact Graph\n  Routing (RFC 9174) or epidemic/PRoPHET multi-hop relay — a bundle that\n  needs more than one hop to reach its destination is not yet routable by\n  this R0.\n\n### Decision 4: actual transport I/O is out of scope here — a future, separate decision\n\nThis ADR does not stand up a mesh-radio driver, a satellite modem\nintegration, or actual internet sockets. `kotoba.dtn.link` records describe\n*what a link looks like*; supplying real ones is a host's job. The\ninternet-overlay leg is expected to compose with the already-existing\n`kotoba-lang/net` (gossip/bitswap semantics) and `kotoba-lang/turn`\n(RFC 8656 relay) libraries rather than reimplementing P2P transport here —\nconsistent with this workspace's layering discipline (`apn` excludes\nphysical-layer optics for the same reason: that's a different library's\njob). Mesh-radio and satellite adapters have no existing kotoba-lang\ncounterpart yet and are explicitly deferred.\n\n### Decision 5: no new cloud-itonami actor in this ADR\n\nNo ISIC vertical cleanly fits \"independent, carrier-non-dependent resilient\nmessaging operator\" — the two adjacent codes already registered\n(`isic-6120` licensed MNO, `isic-6190` VoIP/bandwidth reseller) are both\n*carrier-dependent* by definition, the opposite of this design's premise.\nNew ISIC registrations are a reviewed business decision, not something to\nadd routinely alongside a library ADR (root `CLAUDE.md`'s BMC/ISIC section).\nA consuming actor — e.g. cloud-itonami using `dtn`+`rcs` for its own\nactor↔actor/actor↔user notification delivery to dodge carrier SMS fees, or\na dedicated future infra actor — is left as a separate, later decision,\nmirroring how `kotoba-lang/webrtc`'s own ADR left \"an actual signaling\nserver / consuming actor\" as explicit future work.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `kotoba-lang/rcs`: `clojure -M:test` — 19 tests / 71 assertions, 0\n  failures. `clojure -M:lint` — 0 errors/warnings. Pushed to\n  `github.com/kotoba-lang/rcs` (public), commit\n  `47ea586234ea8bcb286b18b28365d67348ad6d4a`. CI green.\n- `kotoba-lang/dtn`: `clojure -M:test` — 20 tests / 84 assertions, 0\n  failures. `clojure -M:lint` — 0 errors/warnings. Pushed to\n  `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit\n  `34b69b2ce7e5e4ee4abd8a400ab1c4dcbb3edfbd`. CI green.\n- `manifest/repos.edn`/`manifest/west.yml`: both registered via\n  `--entry rcs,dtn` minimal diff (10-line west.yml insert, 1-line\n  repos.edn `:extra-projects` insert), pin-verified\n  (`verify-west-pins: 2 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`, both \"新規 entry, pin\n  ... は main から到達可能\"), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree\n  (not the shared superproject checkout) + GitHub API server-side merge\n  (`repos.edn`'s `:manifest-workflow` canonical path).\n- Pre-existing, unrelated `west.yml is STALE` drift across ~1,700+ other\n  projects (pins that advanced locally without a matching regeneration\n  commit, observed both before and after this ADR's own sync) was\n  deliberately **not** touched — a wholesale regeneration commit is\n  prohibited by root `CLAUDE.md` / `repos.edn`'s own `:manifest-workflow`,\n  and is out of scope for this ADR.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang` gains two genuinely tested, pushed capability libraries\n  (155 assertions combined, 0 lint warnings) implementing the SMS/RCS\n  wire-compatibility half of the design and the transport-agnostic\n  store-and-forward routing half — not blueprint-only placeholders.\n- The interop seam is real and composable today: a `kotoba.phone/sms` (or\n  future `kotoba.rcs` chat-message) record is byte-identical whether it\n  arrives via a carrier gateway or a `kotoba.dtn` bundle, so a single\n  serializer adapter covers both paths.\n- The actual multi-transport wire-up — a mesh-radio adapter, a satellite\n  modem adapter, and wiring the internet-overlay leg through\n  `kotoba-lang/net`/`kotoba-lang/turn` — remains real, undone future work.\n  This ADR closes the *protocol-and-routing-model* gap the user's question\n  identified; it does not itself stand up a running network.\n- No cloud-itonami actor consumes this yet (Decision 5); the libraries are\n  unused in production until a consuming actor or app is built.\n- `kotoba.dtn.router`'s direct-neighbor-or-store scope (Decision 3) is a\n  known, documented gap — genuine multi-hop delay-tolerant routing (CGR or\n  epidemic) is additive future work, not silently claimed here.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **Registering a new cloud-itonami ISIC actor immediately.** Rejected: no\n  ISIC vertical fits an explicitly carrier-independent operator, and new\n  ISIC registration is a reviewed business decision this ADR should not\n  make unilaterally alongside a library design (Decision 5).\n- **`kotoba-lang/dtn` `require`-ing `kotoba-lang/rcs` directly.** Rejected:\n  the two libraries were built independently in parallel specifically to\n  avoid a build-order coupling; a structural/duck-typed gateway seam keeps\n  them independently buildable and testable permanently (Decision 2).\n- **Implementing full RFC 9171 CBOR encoding, BPSec, and Contact Graph\n  Routing in R0.** Rejected in favor of an honestly-scoped, tested\n  direct-neighbor-or-store heuristic — see Decision 3. Claim exactly what\n  the code does, not more.\n- **Building actual mesh-radio/satellite transport adapters as part of\n  this ADR.** Rejected: that is real hardware/ops-integration work\n  requiring its own scoping and (per the workspace's 3D-engine and\n  Rust-crate precedents) should not be improvised inline when the\n  library-layer gap is what was actually asked about — see Decision 4.\n\n## References\n\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/rcs/README.edn` — https://github.com/kotoba-lang/rcs\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/README.edn` — https://github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/phone/README.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607084700-kotoba-lang-apn-cloud-itonami-isic-6110.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607011700-kotoba-lang-webrtc.edn`\n", :adr/artifacts "{:kotoba-lang-rcs \"kotoba-lang/rcs @ 47ea586234ea (pushed, public)\", :kotoba-lang-dtn \"kotoba-lang/dtn @ 34b69b2ce7e5 (pushed, public)\", :manifest \"orgs/kotoba-lang/{rcs,dtn} registered via --entry rcs,dtn, pin-verified\"}", :db/id -1, :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2607161743: kotoba-lang/rcs + kotoba-lang/dtn — a carrier-network-non-dependent telecom substrate, wire-compatible with SMS/RCS", :adr/source-format "edn"}]